// Test: an exec-time resolver on a server type with no `@rootFragment` is an
// error. Nothing schedules it at exec time, so it would never run and the field
// would read `undefined`.

//- FooQuery.js
graphql`query FooQuery($text: String!) @exec_time_resolvers {
  me {
    echo(text: $text)
  }
}`

//- User_echo.js
/**
 * @relayField User.echo(text: String!): RelayResolverValue
 */

//- relay.config.json
{
  "language": "flow",
  "jsModuleFormat": "haste",
  "schema": "schema.graphql",
  "featureFlags": {
    "enable_exec_time_resolvers_directive": true
  }
}

//- schema.graphql
type Query { me: User }
type User { id: ID!, name: String }
==================================== OUTPUT ===================================
✖︎ Resolver `User.echo` is on a server type and is selected in an @exec_time_resolvers operation, but it declares no @rootFragment. At exec time a resolver on a server record runs once per record, scheduled by its root fragment; without one it never runs and the field reads `undefined`. Add a @rootFragment on `User` that selects `id` or `__typename`, or move the field to `Query` if it does not depend on the record.

